window.docContents[237].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models--Weak-waves-and-higher-regularity":{"contents":"The variational wave theory runs on measurable operator fields, Galerkin\napproximation, weak compactness, and an energy argument for uniqueness. A\ndifferentiated Galerkin chain together with elliptic spatial lifts gives\nhigher regularity on the decreasing Sobolev scales in Evans's estimate. The\norder-two nonsymmetric divergence-form lift is concrete. At higher orders,\nthe symmetric existence theorem takes a stable Galerkin scheme, Ritz\northogonality, and elliptic lifts as hypotheses. The nonsymmetric estimate\nassembly takes the two top energy bounds and the spatial recurrence as inputs.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Characteristics, Waves, and Nonlinear Models","header":"7.6.1.3. Weak waves and higher regularity","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models--Weak-waves-and-higher-regularity"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Scope-of-the-integral-theorem":{"contents":"The boundary condition of the affine theorem is \nfor a given Sobolev field . Because its compactness step uses only\nzero-boundary corrections, the theorem holds on every bounded measurable set\nin positive dimension, including dimension one.\n\nEnergies of vector-valued maps that depend on  are not convex in\nthe gradient. For them the lower-semicontinuity step goes through the weak\ncontinuity of the determinant instead, giving the\npolyconvex existence theorem, and\nthe same continuity makes the pointwise constraint  weakly\nclosed. Each further constraint needs its own proof that the admissible set\nis nonempty and weakly closed.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method\u0009Sobolev Integral Functionals","header":"9.3.1.7.2. Scope of the integral theorem","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Scope-of-the-integral-theorem"}});